{%- comment -%}
  Resolves a named motion step to a duration in milliseconds, and prints it.

  The merchant picks a step, not a number — the same rule as space-step.liquid,
  and the same reason: a slider from 60 to 900 in tens is eighty-five values,
  of which about five are distinguishable.

  **This prints a bare number, not a CSS time, and that is deliberate.** The
  caller appends the unit. `--duration` is consumed inside calc() —
  `calc(var(--duration) * 2)` at base.css:3337 and :6245 — so a step that
  resolved to a keyword would make those declarations invalid at
  computed-value time and silently drop the transition rather than fall back.
  Every step setting whose token reaches calc(), color-mix() or clamp() has to
  resolve to a number this way; the shipped precedent is type_ratio_min and
  type_ratio_max, which are selects of numeric strings coerced with
  `| times: 1.0`.

  The rungs are the reference design's own transition durations, by how often
  it uses each:

    quick    250ms   the design's .25s
    base     350ms   the design's .35s — its commonest transition, 41 uses
    relaxed  500ms   the design's .5s, 22 uses
    slow     700ms   the design's .7s
    slowest  900ms   the design's .9s, 12 uses

  Accepts:
    step  - one of the ids above. Anything unrecognised prints nothing so the
            caller's fallback stands.

  Usage:
    {%- capture ms -%}{%- render 'motion-step', step: settings.motion_duration -%}{%- endcapture -%}
    --duration: {{ ms }}ms;
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- case step -%}
  {%- when 'quick' -%}250
  {%- when 'base' -%}350
  {%- when 'relaxed' -%}500
  {%- when 'slow' -%}700
  {%- when 'slowest' -%}900
{%- endcase -%}
